  "description": "Discover non-linear clusters of arbitrary shape and detect noise outliers based on density connectivity.\n\nUnlike K-Means, DBSCAN does not assume spherical clusters and autonomously determines the number of clusters while isolating noise.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nN_\\varepsilon(p) = \\{ q \\in D \\mid \\|p - q\\|_2 \\le \\varepsilon \\}, \\quad |N_\\varepsilon(p)| \\ge \\text{MinPts}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Identify core points having at least min_samples within distance epsilon\n2. Expand density-reachable clusters transitively through density-connected neighbors\n3. Label points outside dense neighborhoods as noise (-1)\n\n### Returns\nList of integer cluster labels (-1 for noise).",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: DBSCAN\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn computational mathematics and machine learning, robust algorithmic routines ensure calculations are both mathematically sound and computationally efficient. **DBSCAN** implements a core computational building block.\n\nUnlike K-Means, DBSCAN does not assume spherical clusters and autonomously determines the number of clusters while isolating noise. In this problem, the goal is to: discover non-linear clusters of arbitrary shape and detect noise outliers based on density connectivity.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nN_\\varepsilon(p) = \\{ q \\in D \\mid \\|p - q\\|_2 \\le \\varepsilon \\}, \\quad |N_\\varepsilon(p)| \\ge \\text{MinPts}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Identify core points having at least min_samples within distance epsilon\n2. **Phase 2**: Expand density-reachable clusters transitively through density-connected neighbors\n3. **Phase 3**: Label points outside dense neighborhoods as noise (-1)\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Algorithmic Complexity**: Designing routines with optimal time and space complexity ensures they scale gracefully to large datasets.\n* **Numerical Accuracy**: Safeguarding calculations against floating-point underflow or division by zero ensures consistent results.\n* **Vectorization**: Implementing operations with contiguous array buffers avoids interpreter overhead.",
